1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:03,093
September twenty-fourth, eighteen sixty-
nine.

2
00:00:03,893 --> 00:00:10,608
On Wall Street, the price of gold is
exploding, and brokers are screaming in
the street as fortunes vanish in minutes.

3
00:00:11,406 --> 00:00:14,423
Men who were rich at breakfast are ruined
by lunch.

4
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:22,183
Behind the chaos, in a quiet office, a
small man with a black beard is watching
the clock and saying almost nothing.

5
00:00:22,982 --> 00:00:28,889
He has just cornered the gold market of
the United States, and he is about to be
hated by an entire country.

6
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:31,376
His name is Jay Gould.

7
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:36,207
And the strangest part of his story is
that he still walked away with the money.

8
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:43,864
Jason Gould was born on December twenty-
seventh, eighteen thirty-six, on a small
farm in upstate New York.

9
00:00:44,664 --> 00:00:49,322
He was a thin, sickly boy with a quiet
voice, and he hated the farm.

10
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:55,064
His father was poor, and Jay learned very
early that nobody respects a poor man.

11
00:00:55,862 --> 00:00:59,973
He worked in a blacksmith's shop, and he
studied at night by candlelight.

12
00:01:00,771 --> 00:01:05,910
When he was fifteen, he had already saved
enough to buy a small set of books on
surveying.

13
00:01:06,709 --> 00:01:11,848
He walked from town to town, measuring
land for farmers, drawing maps of
counties,

14
00:01:11,995 --> 00:01:13,579
and learning who owned what.

15
00:01:14,378 --> 00:01:17,043
This was his first real education in
power.

16
00:01:17,841 --> 00:01:23,124
A man who knows exactly where everything
is can see opportunities that other people
never notice.

17
00:01:23,922 --> 00:01:29,322
At twenty-one, he used his savings and
borrowed money to buy into a tannery in
Pennsylvania.

18
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:32,512
He worked hard, and the business grew.

19
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:37,460
And then his partner, an older man, forced
him out in a bitter fight.

20
00:01:38,258 --> 00:01:43,970
Gould never forgot the lesson: in
business, the person who gets the first
strike usually wins.

21
00:01:44,886 --> 00:01:47,877
Gould moved to New York and discovered the
railroads.

22
00:01:48,677 --> 00:01:52,983
They were the greatest machines of the
age, and most of them were run badly.

23
00:01:53,781 --> 00:02:00,535
He began buying the shares of a small,
failing line, and he learned how to turn
almost worthless paper into power.

24
00:02:01,334 --> 00:02:04,819
Then he found his real battlefield: the
Erie Railroad.

25
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:13,204
In eighteen sixty-eight, the richest man
in America, Cornelius Vanderbilt, decided
to take it over by buying up its stock.

26
00:02:14,003 --> 00:02:15,327
It should have been easy.

27
00:02:16,125 --> 00:02:21,967
Gould and his partners, Daniel Drew and a
flamboyant showman named Jim Fisk, had a
trick.

28
00:02:22,766 --> 00:02:26,342
Whenever Vanderbilt bought shares, they
simply printed more.

29
00:02:27,141 --> 00:02:32,189
The Erie company had a printing press, and
they used it to create new stock out of
thin air.

30
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:36,694
Vanderbilt bought and bought, and the
number of shares kept growing.

31
00:02:37,492 --> 00:02:38,881
He lost millions.

32
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:48,973
Furious, he got a judge to issue arrest
orders, so Gould and his friends grabbed
about six million dollars in cash and fled
across the river to New Jersey,

33
00:02:49,107 --> 00:02:51,590
where they lived in a hotel guarded by
cannons.

34
00:02:52,388 --> 00:02:54,545
In the end, Vanderbilt settled.

35
00:02:55,344 --> 00:03:02,293
The newspapers called it the Erie War, and
a thirty-two-year-old with a soft voice
had just beaten the greatest tycoon in
America.

36
00:03:03,198 --> 00:03:05,251
But Gould was not satisfied.

37
00:03:06,052 --> 00:03:09,264
The next plan was bigger, and far more
dangerous.

38
00:03:10,062 --> 00:03:12,089
He wanted to corner the gold market.

39
00:03:12,888 --> 00:03:14,368
The idea was simple.

40
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:19,055
If he could buy most of the gold in New
York, the price would shoot up.

41
00:03:19,854 --> 00:03:25,761
Farmers in the West, who sold wheat for
export, would benefit, and the government
would not interfere.

42
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:31,360
And all he needed was one thing: the
promise that the Treasury would not sell
gold.

43
00:03:32,159 --> 00:03:38,730
So Gould and Jim Fisk got close to a man
named Abel Corbin, who happened to be the
brother-in-law of the president,

44
00:03:38,864 --> 00:03:40,071
Ulysses Grant.

45
00:03:40,869 --> 00:03:45,943
They gave Corbin gold, they flattered him,
and they used him to whisper in the
president's ear.

46
00:03:46,742 --> 00:03:50,240
In September eighteen sixty-nine, they
started buying.

47
00:03:51,039 --> 00:03:55,657
The price of gold climbed from one hundred
and thirty to one hundred and sixty-two
dollars.

48
00:03:56,455 --> 00:03:59,277
Brokers who had bet against gold were
being crushed.

49
00:04:00,075 --> 00:04:05,996
Then, on the morning of Friday, September
twenty-fourth, Grant finally understood
what was happening.

50
00:04:06,794 --> 00:04:10,266
He ordered the Treasury to sell four
million dollars in gold.

51
00:04:11,065 --> 00:04:12,910
The price collapsed in minutes.

52
00:04:13,708 --> 00:04:18,326
Hundreds of businesses went bankrupt, and
the day became known as Black Friday.

53
00:04:19,125 --> 00:04:20,331
Here is the twist.

54
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:25,006
Gould had secretly sold his own gold the
moment he sensed the danger.

55
00:04:25,804 --> 00:04:32,141
He kept his profit, perhaps ten million
dollars, while Fisk and everyone else
fought off angry mobs.

56
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:34,615
The crowds wanted to hang them.

57
00:04:35,414 --> 00:04:37,493
And Jay Gould had already left.

58
00:04:38,406 --> 00:04:41,866
After Black Friday, any normal man would
have disappeared.

59
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:44,068
Gould did the opposite.

60
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:46,881
He started to collect railroads.

61
00:04:47,679 --> 00:04:52,167
He took control of the Union Pacific, the
great line that crossed the continent.

62
00:04:52,965 --> 00:04:59,342
He bought the Kansas Pacific, then the
Missouri Pacific, and a web of smaller
lines across the Southwest.

63
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:04,927
Within a few years, he controlled about a
ninth of all the railroad track in
America.

64
00:05:05,726 --> 00:05:11,425
He also bought Western Union, the company
that controlled almost every telegraph
wire in the country.

65
00:05:12,223 --> 00:05:16,959
He even bought a newspaper, the New York
World, and used it to shape the news.

66
00:05:17,757 --> 00:05:19,667
His method was always the same.

67
00:05:20,465 --> 00:05:25,240
He bought a weak railroad, made it look
strong, then sold it at a high price.

68
00:05:26,038 --> 00:05:29,680
Or he forced a stronger competitor to buy
him out at a premium.

69
00:05:30,479 --> 00:05:33,625
His boldest move was against Western Union
itself.

70
00:05:34,424 --> 00:05:39,289
Gould built a rival telegraph company, the
American Union, and made it a threat.

71
00:05:40,088 --> 00:05:42,063
Western Union's owners panicked.

72
00:05:42,861 --> 00:05:51,086
In eighteen eighty-one, they let Gould buy
their company, and he walked into the
biggest communications network in the
country almost without paying for it.

73
00:05:51,885 --> 00:05:54,263
He did the same on the streets of New
York.

74
00:05:55,062 --> 00:06:02,571
He bought the Manhattan elevated railways,
the trains that ran above the avenues, and
turned them into a machine that printed
cash.

75
00:06:03,369 --> 00:06:06,490
Every commuter in the city was paying Jay
Gould a nickel.

76
00:06:07,288 --> 00:06:10,175
Critics called him the Mephistopheles of
Wall Street.

77
00:06:10,973 --> 00:06:12,623
He barely seemed to care.

78
00:06:13,421 --> 00:06:19,797
At home, he was gentle and quiet, a man
who grew orchids in a greenhouse and
rarely drank or swore.

79
00:06:20,596 --> 00:06:24,654
The monster in the newspapers was, in
private, almost boring.

80
00:06:25,590 --> 00:06:33,828
Fisk had been Gould's loud partner, and in
eighteen seventy-two, he was shot dead on
the steps of a New York hotel by a rival
over a woman.

81
00:06:34,629 --> 00:06:36,200
Gould lost his shield.

82
00:06:36,998 --> 00:06:41,395
From then on, he stood alone, and the
public hatred came for him alone.

83
00:06:42,194 --> 00:06:49,221
In eighteen eighty-six, the Knights of
Labor, a powerful union, went on strike
against his Southwestern railroads.

84
00:06:50,019 --> 00:06:54,182
For the first time, thousands of workers
were fighting a railroad empire.

85
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:58,322
Gould met the union leaders, and then he
broke the strike.

86
00:06:59,121 --> 00:07:00,718
The union never recovered.

87
00:07:01,517 --> 00:07:08,596
He is also said to have declared, in a
famous line, that he could hire one half
of the working class to kill the other
half.

88
00:07:09,394 --> 00:07:14,468
Historians still argue about whether he
really said it, but millions of people
believed it.

89
00:07:15,267 --> 00:07:17,294
That is how his name was remembered.

90
00:07:18,092 --> 00:07:20,106
There was another side to the hatred.

91
00:07:20,905 --> 00:07:26,864
In eighteen eighty-four, Gould's stock
market fortunes crashed in a panic, and
his enemies celebrated.

92
00:07:27,663 --> 00:07:30,132
He was said to have lost millions in days.

93
00:07:30,931 --> 00:07:33,296
Reporters wrote his obituary early.

94
00:07:34,095 --> 00:07:35,888
Instead, Gould bought.

95
00:07:36,686 --> 00:07:42,294
He waited until panic had pushed stocks to
the floor, and he picked up railroads at
half price.

96
00:07:43,092 --> 00:07:45,549
When confidence returned, he sold them.

97
00:07:46,347 --> 00:07:50,992
It was the same tactic he had used at
Black Friday: let everyone else be afraid,

98
00:07:51,126 --> 00:07:51,981
and be calm.

99
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:56,786
He was a master at one game, and the game
was never about trains.

100
00:07:57,584 --> 00:07:59,065
It was about information.

101
00:07:59,863 --> 00:08:04,768
He knew which stocks were rising, which
governments were weak, and which men would
break.

102
00:08:05,694 --> 00:08:07,357
But he could not buy his health.

103
00:08:08,157 --> 00:08:11,747
By his mid-fifties, Gould was sick with
tuberculosis.

104
00:08:12,545 --> 00:08:16,486
He kept working until the very end, giving
orders from his sickbed.

105
00:08:17,285 --> 00:08:22,242
He died on December second, eighteen
ninety-two, at the age of fifty-six.

106
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:28,765
His fortune was about seventy-seven
million dollars, which made him one of the
richest Americans alive.

107
00:08:29,563 --> 00:08:31,200
The public did not mourn him.

108
00:08:31,998 --> 00:08:36,630
Newspapers wrote that he had done more
harm to the country than any other man of
his time.

109
00:08:37,428 --> 00:08:39,976
And yet his children inherited more than
money.

110
00:08:40,774 --> 00:08:44,351
His son George tried to build the empire
further and failed.

111
00:08:45,149 --> 00:08:51,812
His daughter Helen gave away millions, to
schools, hospitals, and to the veterans of
the Spanish-American War.

112
00:08:52,610 --> 00:08:57,684
Many Americans knew her as the kindest
member of a family that history had
decided to hate.

113
00:08:58,614 --> 00:09:01,214
Jay Gould did not build anything lasting.

114
00:09:02,015 --> 00:09:07,623
He built fortunes out of other people's
fear, and when he died, most of his empire
dissolved.

115
00:09:08,421 --> 00:09:10,357
His story has two lessons.

116
00:09:11,155 --> 00:09:13,482
The first is that information is power.

117
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:22,102
A man who sees the future of one industry,
one law, one election, one hour earlier
than everyone else can win without a
fight.

118
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:24,458
The second is the price.

119
00:09:25,257 --> 00:09:31,516
Gould had wealth, but he had no friends,
no loyalty, and almost no one who would
defend his name.

120
00:09:32,314 --> 00:09:36,685
A fortune built on tricking others can be
large, but it is always fragile.

121
00:09:37,483 --> 00:09:43,300
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe,
because tomorrow at six, another file
opens.

